  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 7,
    "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform Pair Notation",
    "lesson_core_move": "Interpret Fourier transform pair notation by matching $x(t)$ and $X(j \\omega)$ to the forward and inverse transforms that connect a time-domain signal with its spectrum.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform Pair Notation",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Fourier Transform Pair Notation",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The notation $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$ names a Fourier transform pair. It is not asking you to calculate yet; it is saying that one time-domain signal and one frequency-domain spectrum are matched.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The left side, $x(t)$, is the time-domain signal. The variable $t$ tells you that this description is organized by time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The right side, $X(j\\omega)$, is the frequency-domain spectrum. The argument $j\\omega$ tells you that this description is organized by continuous angular frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Reading the pair from left to right gives the forward Fourier transform: start with $x(t)$ and obtain $X(j\\omega)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t) \\xrightarrow{\\mathcal{F}} X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Reading the pair from right to left gives the inverse Fourier transform: start with $X(j\\omega)$ and reconstruct $x(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(j\\omega) \\xrightarrow{\\mathcal{F}^{-1}} x(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For later problems, identify the domain first. If the expression uses $t$, you are looking at the time signal; if it uses $j\\omega$, you are looking at the spectrum. Then read the arrow direction to decide whether the forward or inverse transform is being used.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform Pair Notation",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Matching $x(t)$ with $X(j \\omega)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A reference sheet shows the pair $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$. Identify which symbol is the time-domain signal, which symbol is the spectrum, and state what transform relationship the pair names.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the printed pair $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$. This is a recognition task, not a calculation task: match each symbol to its domain and then read the arrow direction.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The left symbol, $x(t)$, is the time-domain signal because the argument $t$ organizes the description by time. The right symbol, $X(j\\omega)$, is the frequency-domain spectrum because the argument $j\\omega$ organizes the description by continuous angular frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Reading the pair left to right names the forward Fourier transform: start with $x(t)$ and obtain $X(j\\omega)$. Reading the pair right to left names the inverse Fourier transform: start with $X(j\\omega)$ and reconstruct $x(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t) \\xrightarrow{\\mathcal{F}} X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(j\\omega) \\xrightarrow{\\mathcal{F}^{-1}} x(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So when you see a Fourier pair, identify the domain first, then read the arrow direction. $x(t)$ is the time signal, $X(j\\omega)$ is the spectrum, and the notation tells you which transform direction connects them.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform Pair Notation",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Reading the Forward Transform Direction",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "The pair notation $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$ is shown. If you start from $x(t)$, which symbol is produced by the forward transform, and what domain is it in?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the starting symbol $x(t)$. That tells you to read the notation in the signal-to-spectrum direction, not the reverse direction.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the forward transform, $x(t)$ is the input signal and $X(j\\omega)$ is the output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t) \\xrightarrow{\\mathcal{F}} X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The symbol produced by the forward transform is $X(j\\omega)$. Because the argument is $j\\omega$, that output lives in the frequency domain and represents the spectrum.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So if a prompt asks for the forward-transform result from $x(t)$, name $X(j\\omega)$ and identify it as the frequency-domain spectrum.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform Pair Notation",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Reading the Inverse Transform Direction",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "The pair notation $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$ is shown. If you start from $X(j\\omega)$, which function do you recover with the inverse transform, and what domain does it live in?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the starting symbol $X(j\\omega)$. That tells you to read the notation in the spectrum-to-signal direction, not the forward direction.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the inverse transform, $X(j\\omega)$ is the input spectrum and $x(t)$ is the output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(j\\omega) \\xrightarrow{\\mathcal{F}^{-1}} x(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The function recovered by the inverse transform is $x(t)$. Because the argument is $t$, that output lives in the time domain and represents the original signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So if a prompt asks for the inverse-transform result from $X(j\\omega)$, name $x(t)$ and identify it as the time-domain signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform Pair Notation",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting a Fourier Transform Pair Table Entry",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A transform table entry lists $x(t)$ and $X(j\\omega)$ as a paired row. Explain what the row says about the signal, the spectrum, and the transform relationship.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the paired row $x(t)$ and $X(j\\omega)$. Read it as a Fourier pair entry, not as two unrelated symbols.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The left symbol, $x(t)$, is the time-domain signal. The right symbol, $X(j\\omega)$, is the frequency-domain spectrum.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the table row summarizes the same transform relationship from the earlier examples: the forward transform sends $x(t)$ to $X(j\\omega)$, and the inverse transform sends $X(j\\omega)$ back to $x(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When you see this compact table entry, identify the signal, identify the spectrum, and read the row as one paired time-and-frequency description.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
